From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BDK Patches <patches@bdk.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:13:40 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] bdk: allow a Browser in a BDK private profile

OTRProfileID::AllowsBrowserWindows() bans Browsers in every non-primary
off-the-record profile except DevTools, Media Router and (on ChromeOS)
captive portal ones, and Browser::Create() CHECKs that the profile
allows one. BDK's private profiles are each an off-the-record profile of
their own, so that cookies, storage and permissions stay apart between
them (a single primary profile would share them), and BDK keeps every
page as a tab of a Browser (a host context) so Chrome's tab helpers
attach. Add BDK's profile ID prefix to the exceptions. The prefix is
BDK's own and nothing else in Chromium creates a profile with it.

Used by: the BDK engine (//bdk/engine/engine_backend.cc creates the
profile, //bdk/engine/host_context.cc creates its Browser).
---
 chrome/browser/profiles/profile.cc | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/profiles/profile.cc b/chrome/browser/profiles/profile.cc
index 82b9a6f4010bcffe31e2c30c680761dee80c88e9..81ad937dcca67a32c85ab8302f2420318e9d27db 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/profiles/profile.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/profiles/profile.cc
@@ -129,6 +129,12 @@ bool Profile::OTRProfileID::AllowsBrowserWindows() const {
                        base::CompareCase::SENSITIVE)) {
     return true;
   }
+  // BDK's private profiles (//bdk/engine/engine_backend.cc): each is an OTR
+  // profile of its own, and each keeps its pages as tabs of a Browser.
+  if (base::StartsWith(profile_id_, "BDK::Private",
+                       base::CompareCase::SENSITIVE)) {
+    return true;
+  }
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
   if (base::StartsWith(profile_id_, kCaptivePortalOTRProfileIDPrefix,
                        base::CompareCase::SENSITIVE)) {
